{"entries":{"chapter-87-study-constipation|Chapter 87 Study: Constipation":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"definition-amp-epidemiology|Definition & Epidemiology":["u3"],"copy|Rome IV functional constipation requires at least two features: straining, hard or lumpy stool (Bristol 1–2), incomplete evacuation, anorectal blockage, manual maneuvers (each in more than 25% of defecations), or fewer than 3 spontaneous bowel movements/week; criteria must be met during the last 3 months, with onset at least 6 months before diagnosis, rare loose stools without laxatives and insufficient criteria for IBS (Rome Foundation, C2); Hazzard Table 87-1 prints 12 weeks in the last 12 months (p1354).":["u4"],"copy|North American prevalence 12-19%; an estimated 63 million people in North America meet Rome II criteria for constipation (p1354).":["u5"],"copy|Self-reported constipation in adults 65+: 26% women, 16% men; rises to 34%/26% in the 84+ group (p1354).":["u6"],"copy|Normal aging does NOT reduce bowel movement frequency: only 1-7% of any age group report ≤2 BM/wk. Among older adults who self-report \"constipation,\" <10% actually have ≤2 BM/wk and >50% move bowels daily — straining (65%) and hard stool (39%) drive the complaint, not frequency; 21% of community-dwelling people 65+ have rectal outlet delay (p1355).":["u7"],"copy|New-onset constipation incidence in nursing homes screened every 3 months: 7% (p1355).":["u8"],"copy|LTC prevalence is far higher than community: a Finnish study found chronic constipation/rectal outlet delay in 79%/81% (women/men) of nursing home residents; 50-74% of LTC residents use daily laxatives (p1355).":["u9"],"pathophysiology|Pathophysiology":["u10"],"copy|Normal total gut transit <72 h; older adults with chronic constipation average 4-9 days, extending to 6 days to >14 days if bedridden/institutionalized (p1356).":["u11"],"copy|Slowed transit reduces stool water content (normally 75%), shrinking fecal bulk and further blunting propulsive activity — a self-worsening cycle (p1356).":["u12"],"copy|37% of non-constipated older adults were unable to evacuate a small solid sphere in simulated defecation studies (p1357).":["u13"],"copy|Table 87-4 age-related mechanisms of chronic constipation (owner-highlighted): increased colonic collagen deposition, reduced inhibitory nerve input to circular muscle, and increased plasma-endorphin binding to gut receptors (p1356).":["u14"],"risk-factors|Risk Factors":["u15"],"copy|Polypharmacy (≥5 medications) is an established risk factor (p1359); nursing home residents average 6-9 meds/day (p1358). Anticholinergic drugs are the only medication class with level [1] evidence in Table 87-5 (p1359).":["u16"],"copy|Iron sulfate >325 mg/day: does not substantially increase iron absorption in older people and may significantly increase GI side effects (p1359).":["u17"],"copy|Calcium supplementation has a real but modest effect: 13% vs 9.1% placebo over a 5-year trial (p1359).":["u18"],"copy|Parkinson disease: 59% meet Rome criteria (vs 21% in age-matched controls); pelvic dyssynergia affects 60% of PD patients (p1360).":["u19"],"copy|Stroke: 60% of patients are constipated while undergoing rehabilitation (p1360).":["u20"],"copy|Diabetes: colonic transit prolongs to 200 ± 144 h in immobile diabetics. If using metoclopramide, older adults should avoid continuous treatment for longer than 12 weeks, with a 2-week drug holiday or a dose decrease as tolerated (tardive dyskinesia risk) (p1361).":["u21"],"copy|In one adjusted study, reporting <3 BM/week was associated with >2-fold colon cancer risk — constipation itself is not an indication for colonoscopy, but a genuine change in bowel habit should prompt workup (p1361).":["u22"],"complications|Complications":["u23"],"copy|Overflow fecal incontinence in frail nursing home residents: enemas-until-clear then lactulose achieved 94% resolution — yet only 4% of these residents are referred for further assessment (p1361).":["u24"],"copy|Fecal impaction was the primary reason for 27% of admissions in one UK acute geriatric unit survey (p1362).":["u25"],"copy|Constipation → urinary retention: constipation raised the risk of retention (PVR >100 mL) 4-fold in hospitalized women 65+; treating the constipation dropped mean PVR from 85→30 mL over 4 months (p1362).":["u26"],"copy|Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome): cecal diameter ≥10 cm on x-ray (p1362).":["u27"],"copy|Diverticular disease affects 30-60% of adults over 60 (p1363).":["u28"],"evaluation|Evaluation":["u29"],"copy|Digital rectal exam is required in everyone reporting constipation; an empty rectum on exam does NOT exclude the diagnosis (p1364).":["u30"],"copy|Normal perineal descent is <4 cm (p1365). Plain x-ray: colon diameter >6.5 cm without acute obstruction points toward megacolon; rectal dilatation >4 cm implies dysmotility (p1365).":["u31"],"copy|Colonic transit marker study: normal clears to ≤5 of 24 markers by day 5; balloon expulsion normal range 1-5 min (p1368).":["u32"],"copy|Colonoscopy is NOT indicated for constipation alone — in 400 colonoscopies in octogenarians, cancer detection was only 2% for symptoms other than bleeding (eg, constipation, abdominal pain) vs 12% for bleeding (p1368).":["u33"],"nonpharmacologic-management|Nonpharmacologic Management":["u34"],"copy|Fiber target 20-35 g/day; start low at 3-4 g/day and titrate up to limit bloating (p1370).":["u35"],"copy|Fluid 1.5-2 L/day significantly enhanced the effect of added fiber in an RCT in adults aged 18-50; often not practical or acceptable for frail older patients (p1370).":["u36"],"copy|For dyssynergic defecation, biofeedback improved symptoms in 70% vs 23% with diazepam at 3 months; in a separate comparison, 80% vs 22% improved with biofeedback versus laxatives at both 6 and 12 months (p1371).":["u37"],"drugs-name-dose-key-cautions|Drugs (name — dose — key cautions)":["u38"],"copy|Senna — 1-3 tabs at bedtime if symptoms persist after bulk laxatives in ambulant older people (Table 87-11, p1378); onset 8-12 h (p1372); cheap, safe first-line stimulant.":["u39"],"copy|Bisacodyl — 5–10 mg by mouth up to 3 times/wk (Table 87-9, p1373); 10 mg daily was the trial regimen in outpatients with a mean age of 62 (p1375); caution: bisacodyl used for colonoscopy prep may raise ischemic colitis risk in patients with cardiovascular disease (p1368).":["u40"],"copy|Psyllium/bulk agents — 1-3×/day with fluids; adds 1.4 BM/wk vs placebo (p1375); caution: impaction risk if fluid intake is poor.":["u41"],"copy|Lactulose/sorbitol — 15–30 mL once or twice daily (Table 87-9, p1373); adds 1.9 BM/wk (p1376); sorbitol equally effective and cheaper.":["u42"],"copy|PEG — more potent hyperosmolar laxative than lactulose; a trial of PEG plus electrolytes for disimpaction at 1 L (8 sachets)/day × 3 days achieved 89% success (p1376); caution: liquid stools.":["u43"],"copy|Magnesium salts — avoid magnesium hydroxide in CKD; magnesium citrate not recommended in older adults at all (pseudo-obstruction risk) (p1376).":["u44"],"copy|Docusate — avoid: little or no laxative action, even at 300 mg/day (p1376), despite being the predominantly prescribed agent in US nursing homes (p1372).":["u45"],"copy|Glycerin suppository — first-line for rectal outlet delay; bisacodyl suppository second-line; onset 5-45 min (p1377).":["u46"],"copy|Lubiprostone 24 mcg BID; linaclotide; plecanatide (placebo-controlled trials showed efficacy at 3 and 6 mg — trial doses) — secretagogues; effective but costly, with limited older-adult trial data (p1377).":["u47"],"copy|Prucalopride — 5-HT4 agonist; unlike older 5-HT4 agents (cisapride, tegaserod), not associated with major adverse cardiovascular events (p1377).":["u48"],"copy|PAMORAs for opioid-induced constipation — naldemedine (52% vs 35% achieving >3 CSBM vs placebo) and naloxegol (42% vs 29% achieving ≥3 CSBM/wk) (p1378).":["u49"],"copy|Table 87-9 laxative mechanisms (owner-highlighted): sodium picosulfate is hydrolyzed by colonic bacteria, bisacodyl by intestinal/colonic brush-border enzymes — both to the same active metabolite (antiabsorptive/secretory, prokinetic); sorbitol/lactulose are nonabsorbable disaccharides metabolized by colonic bacteria to short-chain fatty acids; PEG osmotically increases intraluminal fluids; senna/anthraquinones alter electrolyte transport via increased intraluminal fluids, stimulate the myenteric plexus, and increase motility (p1373–1374).":["u50"],"recall|Recall":["u51"],"copy|CHAPTER 87s / HAZZARD":["u52"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 87 — Constipation; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u53"],"copy|No exam-trap or book self-contradiction note is flagged in this source chapter.":["u54"],"copy|1.":["u55"],"copy|What Rome IV threshold and time frame define chronic constipation?":["u56"],"copy|At least two features: fewer than three spontaneous bowel movements/week, or straining, Bristol 1–2 stools, incomplete evacuation, blockage or manual maneuvers in more than 25% of defecations; symptoms fulfill criteria for the last three months and began at least six months before diagnosis, loose stools are rare without laxatives, and IBS criteria are insufficient (Rome Foundation, C2); Table 87-1 (p1354) gives a different time window.":["u57"],"copy|2.":["u58"],"copy|Roughly what proportion of North Americans have constipation, and how many meet formal criteria?":["u59"],"copy|12-19% prevalence; about 63 million people in North America meet Rome II criteria (p1354).":["u60"],"copy|5.":["u61"],"copy|How prolonged is total gut transit time in bedridden/institutionalized older adults with constipation, versus normal?":["u62"],"copy|6 days to more than 14 days, versus a normal total transit of under 72 hours (p1356).":["u63"],"copy|8.":["u64"],"copy|What percentage of Parkinson disease patients meet Rome criteria for constipation, versus age-matched controls?":["u65"],"copy|59% vs 21% in controls (p1360).":["u66"],"copy|9.":["u67"],"copy|Why must metoclopramide use in older adults be time-limited, and beyond what duration should continuous treatment be avoided?":["u68"],"copy|Risk of tardive dyskinesia is increased in older adults; avoid continuous treatment for longer than 12 weeks.":["u69"],"copy|A 2-week drug holiday or a decrease of metoclopramide dose as tolerated (with tight blood glucose control) is encouraged whenever clinically possible (p1361).":["u70"],"copy|11.":["u71"],"copy|What PVR cutoff and relative risk link constipation to urinary retention in hospitalized older women?":["u72"],"copy|PVR >100 mL; constipation increased retention risk 4-fold (p1362).":["u73"],"copy|14.":["u74"],"copy|What daily fiber intake does the chapter recommend, and what fluid intake enhanced fiber's effect in trials?":["u75"],"copy|Fiber 20-35 g/day (start at 3-4 g/day and titrate); fluid 1.5-2 L/day enhanced fiber's effect in an RCT in adults 18-50, though often impractical for frail older patients (p1370).":["u76"],"copy|Answer":["u77","u78","u79","u80","u81","u82","u83"],"copy|Table 87-9 laxative mechanisms (owner-highlighted): sodium picosulfate is hydrolyzed by colonic bacteria, bisacodyl by intestinal/colonic brush-border enzymes — both to the same active metabolite (antiabsorptive/secretory, prokinetic); lactulose is a nonabsorbed disaccharide fermented by colonic bacteria, while sorbitol is an osmotic sugar alcohol; PEG osmotically increases intraluminal fluids; senna/anthraquinones alter electrolyte transport via increased intraluminal fluids, stimulate the myenteric plexus, and increase motility (p1373–1374).":["u50"],"copy|The June 2025 pseudo-obstruction option pairs neostigmine with the brand name of a different drug (see Israeli context above).":["u54"],"copy|Diabetes: colonic transit prolongs to 200 ± 144 h in immobile diabetics. Metoclopramide may treat diabetic gastroparesis. Tardive dyskinesia risk rises with total exposure. For diabetic gastroparesis, US prescribing information advises avoiding more than 12 weeks of total treatment. A drug holiday does not reset cumulative exposure (p1361). Reglan prescribing information, Boxed Warning":["u21"],"copy|PAMORAs for opioid-induced constipation — naldemedine and naloxegol counter opioid effects in the gut. Pooled response rates on p1378 are 52% vs 35% and 42% vs 29%, respectively. The pivotal trials measured spontaneous bowel movements, without requiring a sense of complete evacuation. Responders had at least three per week and at least one more than baseline for nine of twelve weeks, including three of the last four. COMPOSE trials · KODIAC trials":["u49"],"copy|Why must metoclopramide use in older adults be time-limited, and what total duration should generally be avoided for diabetic gastroparesis?":["u68"],"copy|Older adults are particularly susceptible to tardive dyskinesia, which may be irreversible.":["u69"],"copy|For diabetic gastroparesis, avoid more than 12 weeks of total treatment.":["u69"],"copy|Interrupting treatment or reducing the dose does not erase previous exposure (p1361 and the Reglan boxed warning above).":["u70"],"copy|Functional constipation. Table 87-1 prints at least two features on >25% of occasions for ≥12 weeks in the last 12 months, including ≤2 bowel movements/week, straining, hard stool, incomplete evacuation, blockage or manual maneuvers (p1354).Current practice (not the book): Rome IV specifies <3 spontaneous bowel movements/week for the frequency item, criteria met over the last 3 months with onset ≥6 months earlier, rare loose stools without laxatives and insufficient IBS criteria. Rome Foundation, C2.":["u4"],"copy|Diabetes: colonic transit prolongs to 200 ± 144 h in immobile diabetics. Metoclopramide may treat diabetic gastroparesis. The book advises avoiding continuous metoclopramide for >12 weeks and encourages a two-week drug holiday or a tolerated dose reduction, with tight glucose control when clinically feasible (p1361).Current practice (not the book): Tardive dyskinesia risk rises with total exposure. For diabetic gastroparesis, US prescribing information advises avoiding more than 12 weeks of total treatment. A drug holiday does not reset cumulative exposure. Reglan prescribing information, Boxed Warning":["u21"],"copy|PAMORAs for opioid-induced constipation. Hazzard reports >3 complete spontaneous bowel movements (CSBMs) for naldemedine (52% vs 35%) and ≥3 CSBMs/week for naloxegol (42% vs 29%) (p1378).Current practice (not the book): The pivotal trials measured spontaneous bowel movements, without requiring a sense of complete evacuation. Responders had at least three per week and at least one more than baseline for nine of twelve weeks, including three of the last four. COMPOSE trials · KODIAC trials":["u49"],"copy|Table 87-9 laxative mechanisms (owner-highlighted): sodium picosulfate is hydrolyzed by colonic bacteria, bisacodyl by intestinal/colonic brush-border enzymes — both to the same active metabolite (antiabsorptive/secretory, prokinetic); the table describes lactulose and sorbitol together as “Nonabsorbable disaccharides” fermented by colonic bacteria [sic — lactulose is a disaccharide; sorbitol is a sugar alcohol]; PEG osmotically increases intraluminal fluids; senna/anthraquinones alter electrolyte transport via increased intraluminal fluids, stimulate the myenteric plexus, and increase motility (p1373–1374).":["u50"],"copy|Treat uncomplicated acute colonic dilatation in stages.":["u118"],"copy|Hazzard starts with bowel rest, flatus-tube decompression, correction of potassium and magnesium disturbances, and treatment of the precipitant.":["u120"],"copy|Persistent dilatation may warrant neostigmine if cardiac contraindications are absent, then endoscopic decompression if necessary.":["u123"],"copy|PEG after resolution can reduce recurrence (p1362).":["u124"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): ASGE advises monitored neostigmine with atropine available, and endoscopic decompression when pharmacologic treatment is contraindicated or unsuccessful.":["u207"],"copy|Table 87-1 prints at least two features on >25% of occasions for ≥12 weeks in the last 12 months, including ≤2 bowel movements/week, straining, hard stool, incomplete evacuation, blockage or manual maneuvers (p1354).":["u208"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Rome IV uses fewer than three spontaneous bowel movements weekly and the last-three-month/onset-six-month window.":["u209"],"copy|Rome Foundation, C2.":["u210"],"copy|Why must metoclopramide use in older adults be time-limited, and what limit and interruption does Hazzard describe for continuous treatment?":["u68"],"copy|Older adults are particularly susceptible to potentially irreversible tardive dyskinesia.":["u69"],"copy|The book advises avoiding continuous metoclopramide for >12 weeks and encourages a two-week drug holiday or a tolerated dose reduction, with tight glucose control when clinically feasible (p1361).":["u211"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The US boxed warning addresses total treatment exposure; a holiday does not reset the 12-week limit.":["u212"],"copy|Reglan prescribing information, Boxed Warning.":["u213"]},"maxId":213,"sequenceOffsets":[[214,-130],[248,-123],[330,0]]}
